  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "For how many values of \\(a\\) is it true that the line \\(y = x + a\\) passes through the vertex of the parabola \\(y = x^2 + a^2?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(10\\)",
    "E": "infinitely many"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "For each fixed real \\(a\\), the parabola \\(y=x^2+a^2\\) is the graph of \\(y=x^2\\) shifted vertically by \\(a^2\\). Its vertex is therefore \\((0,a^2)\\).\n\nFor this point to lie on \\(y=x+a\\), its coordinates must satisfy\n\\[\na^2=0+a.\n\\]\nMoving all terms to one side and factoring gives\n\\[\na^2-a=a(a-1)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so the only possibilities are \\(a=0\\) and \\(a=1\\). Both satisfy \\(a^2=a\\), so both work. There are \\(\\boxed{2}\\) values, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
